## General
- Keep temporary files and experiments out of commit history
- If you spawn a subagent, spawn it with an appropriate effort level

## Coding
- Prefer 2 space indentation unless otherwise specified; match existing indentation if it is not 2 spaces
- Try to keep lines to 80 characters or fewer, it's okay for the occasional line to exceed this limit if it improves readability
- Convert user-facing errors into human-readable descriptions instead of showing raw domains/codes alone (keep the raw code too to maintain precision)

## `git` / `jj`
- Use `jj` instead of `git`. If a repo isn't a jj repo yet, set it up with `jj git init --colocate` (installing `jj` first if it is missing).
- Commit complete and tested units of work using `jj ci`:
  - always pass `-m "<message>"`: without it `jj ci` and `jj split` open an editor and hang
  - the working copy may contain changes you did not make, made by me or other agents working simultaneously: commit only your changes by listing their paths, `jj ci -m "<message>" <paths>`
  - if your change comprises multiple unrelated changes: commit each one separately the same way
  - if your changes share a file with changes that aren't yours, separating them needs the interactive diff editor (`jj split -i`), which you can't drive: ask me rather than committing the whole file
- Don't push unless I explicitly ask. A request to "push" authorizes pushing the current task's tested commit or bookmark to its configured default remote. Ask only when the remote, bookmark, or commits to push are ambiguous.

## Agent configuration
- Store skills in `~/.dotfiles/llm/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and install using `./install`
- Write local agent configuration to `CLAUDE.md` with `AGENTS.md` symlinked to it

## Haskell
- Most of my projects use a `MyPrelude` module that re-exports `ClassyPrelude` and generally useful helper functions
  - When it exists, use it instead of importing `Prelude` or `ClassyPrelude` unless there's a circular dependency
  - Prefer putting generally useful helper functions somewhere in the `MyPrelude.*` module hierarchy
- Most of my Haskell applications use a "God module" architecture: this means having a single library includes all of the code for all executables and tests.
  - This makes it easier to share functionality and improves hot reloading in `ghci`
  - Prefer co-locating tests with the code they test

Refactoring:
- `move-hs-module.sh` (available on PATH) provides a convenient wrapper for renaming modules/moving files

Building:
- Prefer building with `stack` unless the project doesn't have a `stack.yaml` file
  - `stack` projects use hpack which rebuilds the cabal file automatically from `package.yaml`
  - I don't use `stack` for libraries that are published to Hackage, these use `cabal` instead
- Building with `stack`/`cabal` requires making changes to the global package DB so affects files outside of the project. This may run into problems with sandboxing

## iOS / macOS / Xcode / Swift
- Use the `xcrun mcpbridge` MCP server for testing. This may require me to approve a permission prompt in Xcode
- When writing code that maps error codes to names or descriptions (e.g. `CKError`, Core Data, `NSURLError`), consult the framework headers in the active SDK (`xcrun --show-sdk-path`, e.g. `CloudKit.framework/Headers/CKError.h`, `CoreData.framework/Headers/CoreDataErrors.h`) rather than writing from memory. Their doc comments are more detailed than `localizedDescription`; base the descriptions on them and use the SDK constants in the mapping so values are compiler-checked

## Shell Scripting
- Use `set -euo pipefail` in scripts
- Prefer using `#!/usr/bin/env bash` as the shebang for bash scripts
- Prefer writing scripts that are compatible with both bash and zsh

## Python
- Use 4 spaces for indentation
- Use `uv` as the script interpreter for python scripts with dependencies not in python's standard distribution
- Always use `uv` or virtual environments for python dependencies not included in python's distribution
